WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.997
For this module, we use a small synthetic healthcare database.

00:00:03.997 --> 00:00:12.187
The data is intentionally simple enough to understand, but it contains the kinds of messy values that create

00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:12.612
real logic problems.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:19.367
The patient information table includes demographics, contact fields, activity status, consent,

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:25.287
and preferred language. The diagnostics table contains diagnosis, severity, and confirmation information.

00:00:25.287 --> 00:00:30.747
Patient billing contains bill amounts, payments, and payment status.

00:00:30.747 --> 00:00:35.532
Admissions gives us admission and discharge dates.

00:00:35.532 --> 00:00:38.152
There is also a convenience view called patient_case.

00:00:38.152 --> 00:00:44.927
That view combines information from several source tables so we can focus on logic rather than spending the

00:00:44.927 --> 00:00:50.602
whole module on joins. We will return to joins in the next module.

00:00:50.602 --> 00:00:53.887
What makes this dataset useful is the edge cases.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:57.812
Some values are NULL. Some text values are empty.

00:00:57.812 --> 00:01:00.087
Some contain only spaces. One bill amount is exactly zero.

00:01:00.087 --> 00:01:02.772
One payment status is missing.

00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:04.997
One consent value is unknown.

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:07.417
Those are not random annoyances.

00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:14.207
They are the records that help us understand whether our rule is actually robust.

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:21.952
Imagine a production system where an email field contains a space because someone pressed the spacebar and

00:01:21.952 --> 00:01:22.452
saved the form.

00:01:22.452 --> 00:01:29.667
A simple IS NULL test would say that email exists, even though there is no usable address.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:33.952
Or imagine a zero-dollar bill that represents a waived charge.

00:01:33.952 --> 00:01:36.722
Treating it as missing would be incorrect.

00:01:36.722 --> 00:01:43.712
So as we work, think of this database as a controlled laboratory for learning how real-world data behaves.
